WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.090
- [Narrator] In this market environment,

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:04.860
the very mention of one economic term sets

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.710
the big indexes on edge.

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:09.240
- But at this point, the
economy is very strong

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.880
and inflationary pressures are high.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.670
And it is, therefore,
appropriate in my view

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:15.300
to consider wrapping up the taper

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:16.380
of our asset purchases,

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:19.524
which we actually announced
at the November meeting

00:00:19.524 --> 00:00:21.750
perhaps a few months sooner.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:23.710
- [Narrator] That's the
Chairman of the Federal Reserve

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:26.430
in late November, foreshadowing
that the central bank

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:29.210
would speed up tapering or winding down

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:31.680
of the bond-buying program
it launched in 2020

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:34.830
to help fight the
COVID-19 economic crisis.

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:37.440
His words sent a shudder
through the markets.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:38.310
- Taper.
- Tapering.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:39.320
- Tapering.
- Taper.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:41.270
- They should be tapering.

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:42.980
- [Narrator] And it's easy to see why.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:45.900
The Fed's stimulus has
fueled a historic rally

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.480
that has lifted major indexes

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.950
far beyond their pre-pandemic highs.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:53.000
So if the economy is
strong, as Powell said,

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.140
and stimulus has been so
good for stock portfolios,

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:57.803
why are the markets so worried?

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.730
Here's Jerome Powell again.

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:04.880
This time in July of 2020,

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:07.960
amid pandemic shutdowns that
had ground the global economy

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:08.850
to a halt.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:10.880
- At the Federal Reserve,
we remain committed

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.650
to using our tools to do what we can

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:14.643
and for as long as it takes

00:01:14.643 --> 00:01:17.130
to provide some relief and stability.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:19.050
- [Narrator] He was promising in part

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:22.500
to continue purchasing $80
billion of Treasury bonds

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:26.320
and $40 billion of mortgage-backed
securities every month

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.300
to increase the money
supply, encourage lending

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:32.050
and help bring the economy
back from the brink.

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:35.220
This type of stimulus is
called quantitative easing.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:36.860
And it works like this.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:39.340
When the Fed buys securities
on the open market,

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:41.150
it takes them out of circulation,

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:43.980
leaving fewer for everyone
else to complete for.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:46.980
With less supply,
investors bid up the price

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:48.640
of the remaining securities,

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.880
and is always the case with bonds,

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.800
when the price goes up,
the yield goes down.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:55.610
So what does it mean for you?

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:57.340
Since treasuries are a benchmark

00:01:57.340 --> 00:01:58.840
for other types of lending,

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.390
lower yields on long-term Treasury bonds

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:04.000
can mean cheaper corporate
bonds and mortgages,

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.210
which can lead to more
hiring and home buying

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:07.510
throughout the economy.

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:09.870
This helps explain why markets thrive

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:11.260
on quantitative easing,

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:14.040
and why they get jittery
at the mention of tapering.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.400
But there are a few
things to keep in mind.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.700
First, tapering refers
to winding down the rate

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:21.980
at which the Fed adds
to its balance sheet,

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:24.620
not actually reducing the total holdings.

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:26.880
The current plan is to reduce purchases

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.430
by $30 billion a month to end in March.

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:32.490
During this period,
the Fed's balance sheet

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:34.890
will keep growing, just more slowly.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:37.490
Second, winding down then
stimulus puts the Fed

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:39.210
in a position to focus on another

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:42.010
of its imperatives, price stability.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:43.570
Here's the former secretary

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:45.251
of the Treasury Larry Summers talking

00:02:45.251 --> 00:02:47.523
to The Wall Street Journal's Greg Ip.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:53.360
- We're now running with
inflation in the 5, 6% range.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:00.760
And achieving the soft landing
from that without recession.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.180
I think it's not likely to be easy.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:06.150
- [Narrator] Inflation
rose to 6.8% in November

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:07.430
from a year earlier.

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:10.090
The fastest pace since 1982.

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:11.690
A year and a half of stimulus

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:13.970
has driven a rise in household income,

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:16.430
spurring a sharp run-up in home values

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:17.640
and other assets.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:20.030
This has boosted wealth
for many Americans,

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:21.760
fueling stronger demand.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:23.570
Meanwhile, a tight labor market

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:25.420
and lasting supply chain issues

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:26.840
has restricted supply,

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:29.370
leading many businesses to raise prices.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:31.610
This is the big reason Powell is talking

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:33.090
about speeding up the taper.

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:35.310
The sooner the Fed
completes the wind-down,

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:36.920
the sooner it would be able to focus

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:40.500
on bringing inflation back
down to its 2% target.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.120
- We are committed to
our price stability goal.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:45.590
We will use our tools,
both to support the economy

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:47.190
and a strong labor market,

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:48.760
and to prevent higher inflation

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:50.540
from becoming entrenched.

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:52.930
- [Narrator] On Wednesday,
Fed officials signaled

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:55.330
they were prepared to raise
interest rates next year

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:57.500
to help bring down high inflation,

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:01.440
and some more hawkish economists
see a bumpy road ahead.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.530
- I think we've got a very difficult set

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:07.780
of policy management
challenges ahead of us

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:12.530
with a period of entrenched
inflation looking

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:14.880
like the immediate prospect.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:16.730
- [Narrator] But for all
the Fed's preparations,

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:18.930
Powell says it still expects inflation

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:20.360
could retreat on its own.

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:23.040
- Most forecasters, including at the Fed,

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:24.590
continue to expect that inflation

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:27.250
will move down significantly
over the next year

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:30.260
as supply and demand imbalances abate.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:32.120
- [Narrator] With an uncertain road ahead,

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:34.080
the markets will continue watching the Fed

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.036
for any sudden moves.

00:04:36.036 --> 00:04:38.703
(pensive music)

